];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Treating "not software" as "give up on structure"',
    body: 'Every path here still rewards the technical discipline your B.Tech built. You are not abandoning rigor, you are choosing which kind of rigor pays for your next decade.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Picking UPSC or GATE only because of the prestige story',
    body: 'The strong pay and stability are real, but both routes demand one to three years of serious, unglamorous preparation. Know the actual timeline and selection rate before you commit a full year to it.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Assuming an MBA fixes an unclear career direction',
    body: 'An MBA amplifies a clear goal. It rarely creates one. Going in only because engineering "did not work out" produces a weaker outcome than going in with a specific target function in mind.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Chasing product management with zero experience',
    body: 'Almost every real product manager built proof first, as an engineer, analyst, or in a customer-facing technical role. Skipping that step usually means a longer, harder job search, not a shortcut.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Ignoring the AI-exposure question inside each path',
    body: 'Entry-level data cleaning, templated report writing, and routine documentation are being automated fastest. Choose the layer of the work where explaining a decision to an accountable human still matters.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What is the best career after B.Tech other than software?',
    a: 'There is no single best path. GATE-to-PSU work pays well with strong stability for core branches. UPSC ESE or civil services suit people who want policy or administrative impact and can commit years to the exam. MBA and consulting suit engineers who want to move toward business decisions. Data analytics offers the fastest, most accessible entry across any branch. The right choice depends on your work style, family financial runway, and whether you want fast income or a longer, higher-ceiling path.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a non-CSE B.Tech graduate get into data analytics or product roles?',
    a: 'Yes. Most analytics hiring, including firms like Genpact and EXL, evaluates SQL, Excel, and project proof over the specific engineering branch. Product management is harder to enter directly, but mechanical, electrical, and other domain-specific engineers often have an advantage for hardware-adjacent or industry-specific product roles once they have some experience.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is an MBA worth it after B.Tech if I do not want a technical job?',
    a: 'It can be, but only with a clear target: management, consulting, product, operations, or a specific business function. An MBA taken only to escape an unclear technical career path, without a Tier-1 program or a defined goal, often produces a weaker return than expected.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to be from Mechanical, Civil, or Electrical to write UPSC ESE?',
    a: 'Yes, for this specific exam. UPSC ESE currently recruits through Civil, Mechanical, Electrical, and Electronics & Telecommunications engineering branches only. Other branches, including Computer Science, are not eligible for ESE and should look at GATE-PSU routes or the general UPSC Civil Services Examination instead.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does it realistically take to clear GATE and get a PSU job?',
    a: 'Most successful candidates prepare seriously for 8-12 months before their GATE attempt. After a qualifying score, PSU-specific selection (interviews, group discussions, document verification) typically takes another 3-6 months, so budget close to a full year end to end.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is management consulting realistic for a B.Tech graduate without an MBA?',
    a: 'Yes, through Big 4 firms and analyst-level consulting roles, but expect a lower starting salary than the widely quoted MBA-entry figures. The premium consulting salaries usually require a Tier-1 MBA or several years of proven client-facing delivery first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are patent law or technical writing realistic paths for an engineering graduate?',
    a: 'Yes. Patent agent work requires clearing the Indian Patent Agent exam after a science or engineering degree and pays well once qualified, because the pool with both technical depth and legal drafting skill is small. Technical writing is a lower-barrier entry point for engineers who communicate clearly, especially in IT, hardware, and medical-device companies.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I stay in my core branch instead of switching to something else entirely?',
    a: 'Only if the actual demand data supports it. Core branches like Mechanical, Civil, and Electrical have real, active hiring right now through infrastructure growth, renewable energy, and manufacturing expansion, and GATE-PSU pay can match or beat many IT-sector entry salaries. Do not assume "core is dead" without checking current vacancy and salary data for your specific branch first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is an MS abroad a good option if I do not want to work in software?',
    a: 'It depends on the specialization and the goal. Renewable energy, biomedical engineering, and other non-software specializations have real demand abroad, but a generic MS taken only to delay a career decision, without a clear post-study goal, often produces a weak return relative to its cost. Working 1-3 years first, then applying with a specific specialization in mind, generally improves the outcome.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are these non-software paths safe from AI automation?',
    a: 'Not equally. Entry-level data cleaning, templated reporting, and routine technical documentation are already being automated. UPSC ESE and PSU engineering-services roles, senior consulting judgment, product decision-making, and patent drafting stay stronger because they require a human to be accountable for the final call, not just produce a draft or a number.',
  },
